As seen in https://github.com/mongoid/mongoid-scroll/pull/28#issuecomment-1441099111 (2c.), we want to prevent discrepancies between the cursor sort options and the original sort options.

This PR raises a new `Mongoid::Scroll::Errors::DifferentSortFieldsError` in case of mismatch:

```ruby
[1] pry(main)> cursor = Mongoid::Scroll::Cursor.from_record(Feed::Item.first, { field: Feed::Item.fields['_id'] })
=> #<Mongoid::Scroll::Cursor:0x000055dfe8ffe588
 @direction=1,
 @field_name="_id",
 @field_type="BSON::ObjectId",
 @tiebreak_id=BSON::ObjectId('541014e17aa58d8ccf000025'),
 @value=BSON::ObjectId('541014e17aa58d8ccf000025')>

[2] pry(main)> Feed::Item.desc(:created_at).scroll(cursor)
Mongoid::Scroll::Errors::DifferentSortFieldsError: 
```

It was inspired from `raise_multiple_sort_fields_error if multiple_sort_fields?`.

```
class procedure TCastleApplicationIdle.ApplicationIdle(Sender: TObject; var Done: Boolean);
...
begin
  ...
  { Call DoUpdate for all TCastleControl instances. }
  for I := 0 to ControlsList.Count - 1 do
  begin
    C := ControlsList[I] as TCastleControl;
    C.DoUpdate;
  end;
  ...
end; 
```
In `C.DoUpdate`, `Invalidate` will be called, and `InvalidateRect` winAPI will be called on windows. The `ApplicationIdle` method will be called **thousands of times** per second (about 6000 times on my computer), exhausting the CPU.
I only used a simple Timer dedicated to handling `Invalidate`, and the cpu usage of the editor is close to 0% after testing.

This removes the snapshot functionality and replaces it with backup and restore that have options which are mixed into the service options allowing it to be configured in place. 

Currently only `geth`, `prysm-beacon` and `nethermind` support backup and restore, with automated testing in place for only Geth at the moment. I have manually tested with Geth and Prysm however this PR is getting bigger and bigger. I want to get this merged and tackle the following up tasks in smaller steps such as #144 and #50 and start iterating based on production feedback.

There is a lot more to document than is being included in this PR description. I want to tackle that more thoroughly as part of #50.

## Backup

When enabled a few things happen:

1. A metadata service `{name}-metadata.service` is created (e.g. `geth-sepolia-metadata.service`) which is managed by `{name}-metadata.timer` which polls the client every 10 seconds for information related to chain height. This is stored within the backup metadata folder `.backup` inside the state directory. 
2. A backup service `{name}-backup.service` is created which is managed by `{name}-backup.service` and runs on the configured schedule with a default of every 24 hours.

There are two types of backups which can be performed when `{name}-backup.service` is triggered:

1. In-situ backup
2. BTRFS snapshot based backup

In the case of an in-situ backup the client service is stopped and it's state directory is backed up via [borgbackup](https://www.borgbackup.org/). When the backup is complete the service is restart.

For a BTRFS snapshot based backup the client service is restarted, forcing a btrfs read-only snapshot to be created in the configured snapshot directory. That directory is then traversed and each snapshot found is backed up if it has not already been. Any snapshots older than a configurable number of days is then deleted to prevent a large number of snapshots being built up. 

When a backup is being created a content hash is created which is later used by the restore process to ensure the integrity of a given backup. 

```nix
services.ethereum.geth-sepolia = {
	backup = {
    	enable = true;

      	# btrfs = {
        #	 enable = true;
        # 	 snapshotDirectory = "/ethereum/snapshots/geth-sepolia";
      	# };

      	borg = {
        	repo = "ssh://borg@dione/data/borgbackup/ethereum/geth-sepolia";
        	keyPath = sops.secrets.borg_backup_key.path;
        	encryption.mode = "none";
        	strictHostKeyChecking = false;	
        	unencryptedRepoAccess = true;
      	};

      schedule = "0/12:00:00";	# every 12 hours
    };
}
```

## Restore

When restore is enabled an `ExecStartPre` script is added to the client's systemd service which will pull a configured snapshot from a remote borg backup repository and unpack it into the service's state directory. This will only occur if the state directory is empty and if the content hash is correct after unpacking.

```nix
services.ethereum.geth-sepolia = {
	restore = {
    	enable = true;
		snapshot = "1785690";

      	borg = {
        	repo = "ssh://borg@dione/data/borgbackup/ethereum/geth-sepolia";
        	keyPath = sops.secrets.borg_backup_key.path;
        	strictHostKeyChecking = false;	
        	unencryptedRepoAccess = true;
      	};
    };
}

```

Closes #72

Setting `padding` of `View` instance after creation corrupts center coordinates when using `useGeographic()`.
```js
useGeographic();
const view = new View({
  center: [135, 35],
  zoom: 6,
});

const center = view.getCenter();
// [ 135, 34.999999999999986 ]

view.padding = [0, 0, 0, 0];

const point = view.getCenter();
// [ 0.001212725633561354, 0.0003144103494321371 ]
```
This should be `center == point` roughly because there is no change about the padding.

This PR fixes the bug, and includes a test for it.
